<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Proposal 44 execution plan, C0 - THE PUPPET'S KINEMATIC CHAIN.

  WHY THIS EXISTS. The shipped puppet rotated the neck about the pelvis
  correctly and then built EVERY segment above it in WORLD coordinates from
  the neck's new position:

      headC     = rotateAbout( neck, QPointF( neck.x(), neck.y() - r ), nod );
      shoulderL = QPointF( neck.x() - shoulderW, neck.y() + torsoLen*0.06 );

  `(neck.x, neck.y - r)` is straight up on the SCREEN, not along the torso
  axis, so the head, the shoulder bar and both arms inherited the neck's
  TRANSLATION and none of its ROTATION. A torso at 20 degrees with a
  bolt-upright head and a perfectly level shoulder bar is a figure with no
  spine - the head reads as gimbal-mounted. That is the defect a user
  reported as "the head is not subordinate to the bending of the torso", and
  it is a DRAWING bug: it waits on no analysis change and no plant.

  WHY THE VERB TAKES EXPLICIT JOINT SCALARS. Nothing in this repo can drive
  an analysis-derived pose to a chosen angle: the pose is a pure function of
  decoded aspects, `assert-feel-flow-pose` only READS it, and no fixture can
  be authored to land `sway` on exactly 0.5 of full scale. So the gate
  supplies the geometry's own inputs and asserts the geometry - a unit gate
  reached through the verb. The painter and this verb call ONE function
  (`sFeelFlowSkeletonFor`), which is the discipline proposal 41 M7 paid for
  with `tagChipRect()` and the take-lane fix paid for again with
  `fillBodyByMaterial()`: two implementations of one layout drift, and they
  drift silently.

  THE ANGLES ARE RECOVERED FROM THE POINTS, never echoed back from the
  inputs that produced them. An assertion against a number the function was
  TOLD would be tautological; one recovered from the resulting geometry
  fails when the construction is wrong.

  MEASURED (2026-08-28; re-pinned 2026-08-31 for proposal 44 C7's plane
  decision), default 200x400 box.

  THE PLANE IS NOW A FIELD, not a compile-time constant, and this case is why.
  C7 moved the `sway` scalar to the SAGITTAL plane on the requester's decision.
  Done as a constant it would have cost two 3D-composition gates: with sway
  sagittal it is COPLANAR with trunkFlex, so the pair merely adds (20 + 25 =
  45.0000, measured) and the cross-term proving that rotations compose as
  ROTATIONS rather than as numbers - 21.8802 / 25.0000 / -8.7447 - has
  nothing left to measure; likewise the arm's 36.6915 collapses to 20 + 35.
  Those pins are the whole difference between a 3D body and two flat drawings
  stacked. `SFeelFlowJoints::sagittalTrunk` keeps both planes reachable, so
  every one of them survives verbatim under plane="lateral" while the SHIPPED
  default is sagittal.

    plane="sagittal" (the default)
      sway= 0.5           trunkFlex 10.0000  headStubFlex 10.0000  armSwingL/R 10.0000
      sway= 1.0           trunkFlex 20.0000  headStubFlex 20.0000  armSwingL/R 20.0000
      sway=-1.0           trunkFlex-20.0000  headStubFlex-20.0000  armSwingL/R-20.0000
      sway=1 nod=1 arm=1  trunkFlex 20.0000  headStubFlex 30.0000  armSwingL 55.0000 armSwingR -15.0000
      sway=1 twist=1      trunkFlex 20.0000  twist -18.8817  shoulderBar -7.0960

    plane="lateral" (retained so the 3D cross-terms stay measurable)
      sway=1 flex=1       trunk 21.8802  trunkFlex 25.0000  twist -8.7447
      sway=1 arm=1        armSwingL 36.6915
      sway=1 nod=1 arm=1  trunk 20.0000  headStub 30.0000

  THE INHERITANCE CHECK IS PLANE-AWARE. It asserts the DRIVEN plane's chain -
  {head stub, both arms} sagittally, {head stub, shoulder bar, both arms}
  laterally - and additionally that the other two planes stay at zero.

  THAT LAST PART IS NOT "STRICTLY STRONGER", THOUGH IT WAS WRITTEN THAT WAY
  FIRST AND THEN MEASURED. A 0.4 degree lateral leak injected on a sagittal
  command fails this case WITH the off-plane assertion (row 19) and WITHOUT it
  (row 16), because section (3)'s explicit three-axis rows already pin all
  three readouts for those commands. What it genuinely adds is coverage on the
  inheritTol rows, which otherwise assert one axis each: a leak that appeared
  only at sway=0.5 or in the small box would have nothing else to trip. Kept on
  that basis, which is a weaker one than first claimed.

  NO SAGITTAL SHOULDER-BAR ROW, and that is geometry rather than an omission:
  the bar lies ALONG the axis sagittal flexion rotates about, so a forward lean
  carries it forward without turning it. Three rows sagittally, four laterally.

  WATCHED FAILING. Reverted `sFeelFlowSkeletonFor` to compose the head,
  shoulders and arms against 0 instead of the trunk angle (i.e. the shipped
  world-coordinate construction): every `inheritTol` row FAILS. The sway=0 row
  still passes, as it must - at zero lean the broken and the correct
  construction coincide, which is exactly why the case sweeps five angles
  rather than asserting one. Separately: flipping the plane WITHOUT the
  plane-aware check made eight rows pass while measuring nothing (0 against 0),
  which is what forced `headStubFlexDeg` and the off-plane assertion.

  NOT GATED. What the figure LOOKS like (aesthetics are not gated anywhere in
  this feature); the head still follows the trunk RIGIDLY, with no neck lag, no
  counter-rotation and no inertia - that is C4's `m*d*a_base` term, and C7
  measured why it cannot simply be bolted onto the drawn series (the trunk's
  own second derivative there is ~640x the real motion, all envelope noise); and
  the pose numbers themselves, which this case does not touch
  (`feel_flow_puppet.qxa` still pins those).
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="feel_flow_puppet_chain">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <!-- (1) THE CHAIN, IN THE SHIPPED (SAGITTAL) PLANE. With the head's own
         nod and the arms' own swing at zero, every segment above the neck must
         carry EXACTLY the trunk's lean. Five angles including zero and both
         signs: at sway=0 the broken construction and the correct one agree, so
         a single-angle assertion could not tell them apart. -->
    <assert-puppet-skeleton sway="0"    nod="0" arm="0" expectFlex="0"   inheritTol="0.01"/>
    <assert-puppet-skeleton sway="0.5"  nod="0" arm="0" expectFlex="10"  inheritTol="0.01"/>
    <assert-puppet-skeleton sway="1.0"  nod="0" arm="0" expectFlex="20"  inheritTol="0.01"/>
    <assert-puppet-skeleton sway="-0.5" nod="0" arm="0" expectFlex="-10" inheritTol="0.01"/>
    <assert-puppet-skeleton sway="-1.0" nod="0" arm="0" expectFlex="-20" inheritTol="0.01"/>

    <!-- (1b) AND THE SAME CHAIN LATERALLY, so the plane switch itself is
         exercised rather than being a constant nobody can reach. Identical
         numbers in the other plane's columns - which is the point: the
         construction is a rotation, and a rotation does not care which axis. -->
    <assert-puppet-skeleton plane="lateral" sway="1.0"  nod="0" arm="0"
                            expectTrunk="20"  inheritTol="0.01"/>
    <assert-puppet-skeleton plane="lateral" sway="-0.5" nod="0" arm="0"
                            expectTrunk="-10" inheritTol="0.01"/>

    <!-- (2) THE COMPOSITION IS A SUM, in the plane each DOF acts in. The head's
         nod is sagittal like the trunk's lean, so it ADDS: 20 + 10. The arms
         are sagittal too, so their own 35 adds and subtracts in antiphase. -->
    <assert-puppet-skeleton sway="1.0" nod="1.0" arm="1.0" expectFlex="20"
                            contains="headStubFlex=30.0000 armSwingL=55.0000 armSwingR=-15.0000"/>

    <!-- (2b) 36.6915, NOT 35 - retained under plane="lateral" because it is
         exactly the pin the plane move would otherwise have destroyed. The arm
         is commanded 35 degrees in the TRUNK's frame while the trunk leans 20
         LATERALLY, so its world-space sagittal angle is
         atan2(sin35, cos35*cos20) = 36.6915. Two flat drawings stacked read 35.
         With both in the sagittal plane the same row reads a plain 55. -->
    <assert-puppet-skeleton plane="lateral" sway="1.0" arm="1.0"
                            expectSwingL="36.6915" tol="0.001"/>
    <assert-puppet-skeleton plane="lateral" sway="1.0" nod="1.0" arm="1.0"
                            expectTrunk="20" contains="headStub=30.0000"/>

    <!-- (3) THE THREE ANATOMICAL AXES ARE DISTINCT. Each rotation moves its own
         plane and leaves the other two alone. The requester's stated core
         interaction - bending FORWARD - is the first row now, which is the
         whole of C7. -->
    <assert-puppet-skeleton sway="1.0"  expectTrunk="0"  expectFlex="20" expectTwist="0"/>
    <assert-puppet-skeleton flex="1.0"  expectTrunk="0"  expectFlex="25" expectTwist="0"/>
    <assert-puppet-skeleton twist="1.0" expectTrunk="0"  expectFlex="0"  expectTwist="-20"/>
    <assert-puppet-skeleton plane="lateral" sway="1.0"
                            expectTrunk="20" expectFlex="0" expectTwist="0"/>

    <!-- (3b) AND THEY COMPOSE AS ROTATIONS, NOT AS NUMBERS - in BOTH planes,
         which is what the field buys. Laterally: 20 plus a sagittal 25 gives
         21.8802 and 25.0000 with a twist of -8.7447 nobody asked for.
         Sagittally: 20 plus an axial 20 gives a twist of -18.8817, NOT -20, and
         tilts the shoulder bar -7.0960 out of a plane neither command names.
         A componentwise implementation reads clean round numbers for both and
         looks entirely plausible. -->
    <assert-puppet-skeleton plane="lateral" sway="1.0" flex="1.0"
                            expectTrunk="21.8802" expectFlex="25.0000"
                            expectTwist="-8.7447" tol="0.001"/>
    <assert-puppet-skeleton sway="1.0" twist="1.0"
                            expectFlex="20.0000" expectTwist="-18.8817" tol="0.001"
                            contains="shoulderBar=-7.0960"/>

    <!-- (3c) sway and trunkFlex are COPLANAR in the shipped plane, so they add
         to exactly 45. Pinned because it is the reason the lateral rows above
         have to exist at all. -->
    <assert-puppet-skeleton sway="1.0" flex="1.0" expectFlex="45.0000" tol="0.001"/>

    <!-- (3d) The arms hang from the trunk, so a lean carries them with it even
         with no swing commanded. -->
    <assert-puppet-skeleton flex="1.0" arm="0" expectSwingL="25.0" tol="0.001"/>

    <!-- (4) SCALE-FREE. The same lean out of a box less than half the size in
         both axes - it is a rotation, not a pixel coincidence. -->
    <assert-puppet-skeleton sway="0.5" nod="0" arm="0" boxW="90" boxH="140"
                            expectFlex="10" inheritTol="0.01"/>

    <!-- (5) THE DEGENERATE BOX still answers, and answers INVALID rather than
         dividing by a zero-height figure. -->
    <assert-puppet-skeleton sway="1.0" boxW="4" boxH="4" expectValid="0" contains="valid=0"/>

  </actions>
</SActionScript>
